import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int arr[] = {1, 2, 3};
        permutation(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
    }

    public static void permutation(int arr[], int l, int r) {
        if (l == r)
            System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));
        else {
            for (int i = l; i <= r; i++) {
                arr = swap(arr, l, i);
                permutation(arr, l + 1, r);
                arr = swap(arr, l, i);
            }
        }
    }

    public static int[] swap(int arr[], int i, int j) {
        int temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = temp;
        return arr;
    }
}
```

In this program, the `permutation` function is a recursive function that generates all permutations of the array. It swaps each element with every other element in the range `l` to `r` and recursively calls itself with the modified array. The `swap` function is a helper function that swaps two elements in the array. The `main` function creates an array and calls the `permutation` function to print all permutations.

